      "text": "## Rhetoric vs. Logic vs. Poetics"
    },
    {
      "id": "00-OVERVIEW.md#027",
      "path": [
        "00-OVERVIEW.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:rhetoric:00-overview, git-history:rhetoric:00-overview]",
        "canonical_path": "rhetoric/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "concepts": "[overview]",
        "current_path": "rhetoric/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "id": "maxim:rhetoric:overview",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "rhetoric",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[overview]",
        "section": "rhetoric",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Rhetoric - Overview and Landscape",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "```\n+---------------------+------------------+--------------------+\n| LOGIC               | RHETORIC         | POETICS            |\n+---------------------+------------------+--------------------+\n| Studies: validity   | Studies:         | Studies: how       |\n| of inference        | persuasion of    | language creates   |\n| regardless of       | specific         | aesthetic effects  |\n| audience            | audiences        |                    |\n+---------------------+------------------+--------------------+\n| Audience: none      | Audience: central| Audience: reader   |\n| (purely formal)     | — defines the    | — may not be       |\n|                     | rhetorical task  | considered at all  |\n+---------------------+------------------+--------------------+\n| Goal: truth/        | Goal: action or  | Goal: pleasure,    |\n| validity            | belief (in the   | insight, aesthetic |\n|                     | audience)        | experience         |\n+---------------------+------------------+--------------------+\n| Method: deduction,  | Method: ethos,   | Method: imagery,   |\n| induction,          | logos, pathos;   | narrative,         |\n| formal proof        | topoi, figures   | metaphor, form     |\n+---------------------+------------------+--------------------+\n| Tools: syllogism,   | Tools: enthymeme,| Tools: prosody,    |\n| truth tables,       | example, topics, | tropes, devices,   |\n| proof systems       | figures, canon   | narrative structure|\n+---------------------+------------------+--------------------+"
    },
    {
      "id": "00-OVERVIEW.md#028",
      "path": [
        "00-OVERVIEW.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:rhetoric:00-overview, git-history:rhetoric:00-overview]",
        "canonical_path": "rhetoric/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "concepts": "[overview]",
        "current_path": "rhetoric/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "id": "maxim:rhetoric:overview",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "rhetoric",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[overview]",
        "section": "rhetoric",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Rhetoric - Overview and Landscape",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "KEY: Aristotle wrote the Organon (logic), the Rhetoric,\n     and the Poetics as three distinct disciplines.\n     The three overlap (logical arguments in rhetoric;\n     rhetorical figures in poetry; emotional appeals\n     in logic about what is worthy of belief) but\n     are analytically distinct.\n```"
    },
